\description{
Renders the classic 2D "squiggle plot" of an RNA secondary structure --
backbone, base pairs, loops -- and optionally paints open reading frames onto
the backbone as coloured highlights, with a separate colour per strand.  This
makes it possible to see directly where a noncanonical ORF (for example a
mitochondrial-derived peptide) sits relative to the stems and loops of the
RNA that encodes it, and whether it reads in the sense or antisense
direction.
}
\details{
\subsection{Dependency}{

The 2D layout is computed by ViennaRNA's \code{RNAplot} program, which must be
installed and on the system \code{PATH}; see \code{\link[=fold_rna]{fold_rna()}} for installation notes.
Only the coordinates are taken from \code{RNAplot} -- all drawing is done with R
graphics, so the result composes with \code{par()}, \code{layout()} and any graphics
device.
}

\subsection{Strand conventions}{

\code{strand_colors} is keyed on the \code{strand} column of \code{orfs}, which for
\code{\link[=scan_orfs]{scan_orfs()}} is \code{"+"} for the given sequence and \code{"-"} for its reverse
complement.  In vertebrate mitochondrial genomes as deposited (e.g. the rCRS)
the \code{"+"} strand corresponds to the heavy (H) strand and \code{"-"} to the light
(L) strand, so the default labels name both conventions.

ORFs that wrap the origin of a circular genome (\code{wraps_around == TRUE})
cannot be drawn as a single stretch of backbone and are dropped with a
warning.
}

\subsection{Naming ORFs}{

When \code{orfs} carries an \code{mdp_name} column -- as produced by
\code{\link[=annotate_mdp_orfs]{annotate_mdp_orfs()}} -- named ORFs are drawn in a per-MDP colour and
labelled on the figure, while ORFs that matched nothing stay in the muted
strand colours as context.  This is the distinction the plot usually needs to
make: in the human 16S every ORF above 60 nt is a known MDP, whereas in the
12S only one of twenty-one is, and the reader has to be able to tell which.
Without an \code{mdp_name} column the function behaves exactly as before, colouring
purely by strand.
}
